   "text": "I read the structure in segments and wrote each as SMILES, then joined them in the order drawn.\n\n1. **Left end:** a 4,4-difluoropyrrolidine carrying a 2-cyano group on a wedge bond. Working through the CIP priorities (N > CN > ring CH2 > H), the drawing gives the S configuration, and I wrote the SMILES to match. The ring nitrogen is acylated by a glycine unit.\n2. **Quinoline:** the glycine NH is acylated by the 4-carboxamide of a quinoline. The 8-position carries an NH linked to a succinyl group, NC(=O)CCC(=O).\n3. **Peptide chain:** the succinyl group continues into Asp, Lys, Asp and Cys. I took all four as L-amino acids, as the wedges and hashes suggest. Lys keeps its free side-chain NH2, and each Asp keeps a free side-chain CO2H. The Cys carboxyl is a free CO2H.\n4. **Cys sulfur:** the thiol sulfur is bonded to C3 of a succinimide ring. That carbon is drawn with plain bonds, so I gave it no stereo label.\n5. **Succinimide to fluorescein:** the succinimide nitrogen is bonded to the fluorescein benzo ring.\n6. **Fluorescein:** a spiro lactone with the 3,6-dihydroxyxanthene. The succinimide-bearing aryl carbon sits next to the CH that is adjacent to the lactone carbonyl-bearing carbon, and I wrote the ring closures to reproduce that.\n\nI checked ring-closure numbering, valences and the position of the aryl attachment against the drawing.\n\nSMILES: N#C[C@@H]1CC(F)(F)CN1C(=O)CNC(=O)c1ccnc2c(NC(=O)CCC(=O)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CCCCN)C(=O)N[C@@H](CC(=O)O)C(=O)N[C@@H](CSC3CC(=O)N(C3=O)c3ccc4c(c3)C(=O)OC45c3ccc(O)cc3Oc3cc(O)ccc35)C(=O)O)cccc12"
